# Go error wrapping

When a function returns an error from another call, wrap it with `fmt.Errorf` using `%w` and a short contextual message describing what failed. Never return a bare upstream error.

```go
// ❌ BAD — bare return
if err != nil {
    return err
}

// ❌ BAD — no context
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("%w", err)
}

// ✅ GOOD — context + error chain preserved
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("fetching secure value: %w", err)
}
```

## Guidelines

- Use `%w` (not `%v`) so callers can use `errors.Is` and `errors.As`.
- Keep messages lowercase, concise, and action-oriented (e.g. `"inserting row: %w"`, `"watch, get latest resource version: %w"`).
- Wrap at each layer that adds meaningful context; avoid repeating the same message at every level.
- Returning a package-level sentinel error (e.g. `ErrNotFound`) is fine when that sentinel is the intended API contract — do not wrap it again just to add noise.
